The difference between vulnerable and safe rendering is usually one function call, not a rewrite:

```javascript
// Vulnerable: raw insertion, browser parses it as HTML
element.innerHTML = userComment;

// Safe: treated as text content, never parsed as markup
element.textContent = userComment;

// Vulnerable: template literal concatenated straight into HTML
const html = `<p>${userComment}</p>`;
container.innerHTML = html;

// Safe: modern templating engines auto-escape by default
// (React JSX, Vue templates, Django/Jinja2 {{ }} all HTML-encode automatically)
function Comment({ text }) { return <p>{text}</p>; } // React encodes `text` automatically
```

Most XSS in modern codebases comes from *opting out* of a framework's default escaping — `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` in React, `v-html` in Vue, the `|safe` filter in Jinja2 — usually to render user-authored rich text (a blog post with bold and links). Those escape hatches need a dedicated HTML sanitizer library, not manual encoding and not blind trust.
